{
  // The dev preview under apps/preview-soundboard.
  //
  // A SEPARATE project on purpose. tsconfig.build.json proves that the shipped
  // audio toolkit is platform-free — it inherits `types: []` and a `lib`
  // WITHOUT "DOM", so a Node global or a browser type leaking into `domain/`
  // fails the build. That proof is the reason the whole suite runs in Node with
  // no jsdom and no AudioContext (docs/testing.md §2), and it is what
  // domain/webaudio-surface.ts was written to preserve while still shipping an
  // adapter. The preview renders to a terminal and needs Node's stdio, so it
  // gets its own project and tsconfig.build.json is left exactly as it was.
  //
  // It also includes `test/fake-webaudio.ts`, which is unusual and deliberate.
  // The preview drives the REAL adapter against the SAME test double the tests
  // use, so the node graph and the automation calls it prints are what the
  // adapter actually did — evidence, not a second description of it that could
  // drift. A preview with its own private re-implementation of the browser
  // would be able to draw a graph the adapter never built.
  //
  // NOTE what this file does NOT do: it does not add "DOM" to `lib`. The
  // preview cannot make a sound and does not try; see apps/preview-soundboard
  // /README.md on what it can and cannot show. A browser soundboard belongs
  // beside this one eventually, not instead of it — it would be the only way to
  // hear anything, and the only way to be unable to grep the result.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["apps/**/*.ts", "src/index.ts", "src/domain/**/*.ts", "test/fake-webaudio.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "coverage", "dist", "test/fixtures/**"]
}
